y and historical precedent of these problems. So perhaps lower probabilities, ensuring that each deadline's probability is higher but not excessively so.\n\nFor example:\n\n- 2026: 5%\n- 2030: 10%\n- 2035: 20%\n- 2040: 30%\n- 2050: 40%\n\nBut the initial response had higher probabilities. Maybe considering that AI could accelerate the process, I should adjust upwards. But to account for the difficulty, not too high.\n\nAlternatively, using a different approach: suppose each problem has a certain chance. There are six problems. If each has, say, a 5% chance by 2050, the combined chance could be higher. But I need to model it correctly.\n\nWait, perhaps using the Poisson process or something similar, but it's complicated. Alternatively, using an approximate calculation.\n\nBut perhaps it's better to just go with the initial thought, as the initial estimate had 2026 at 5%, 2030 at 10%, 2035 at 20%, 2040 at 30%, and 2050 at 40%. But the previous response was 2026: 0.05, 2030: 0.1, 2035: 0.15, 2040: 0.2, 2050: 0.3, which seems consistent.\n\nAlternatively, another way: If I estimate that by 2050, there's a 40% chance, and it's non-decreasing, perhaps:\n\n- 2026: 0.05\n- 2030: 0.1\n- 2035: 0.15\n- 2040: 0.25\n- 2050: 0.4\n\nBut I think the initial approach was better. Considering all factors, the probabilities should increase gradually as AI becomes more advanced and integrated into mathematical research.\n</think>\n\n{\n    \"2026\": 0.05,\n    \"2030\": 0.10,\n    \"2035\": 0.15,\n    \"2040\": 0.25,\n    \"2050\": 0.40\n}",
